 scene:'History: Philly soul (Gamble & Huff, the Sound of Philadelphia, Sigma Sound), John Coltrane\'s house is in Strawberry Mansion, Sun Ra\'s Arkestra still lives communally in Germantown, the Roots came out of the Philadelphia High School for the Creative and Performing Arts, and there is a jazz lineage (the Heath brothers, McCoy Tyner, Christian McBride) that Chris\' Jazz Cafe and South still trade on.\n\nThe last fifteen years: an indie/DIY explosion out of West Philly and Fishtown basements: Kurt Vile, The War on Drugs, Japanese Breakfast, Alex G, Hop Along, Sheer Mag, Mannequin Pussy, Dr. Dog, Waxahatchee (moved here), all-ages church-basement shows (R5 at First Unitarian), and a venue ecosystem that punches far above the city\'s size: Johnny Brenda\'s (250 cap, the one that started Fishtown), Union Transfer (1,200, the best mid-size room on the East Coast), Underground Arts, Kung Fu Necktie, Ortlieb\'s, PhilaMOCA, the Fillmore/Foundry for bigger names, the Mann amphitheatre in the park in summer, and the Met, a restored 1908 opera house, for arena-adjacent acts.\n\nThe experimental end: Ars Nova Workshop\'s Solar Myth (natural wine bar in front, free-jazz and new-music room in the back: the closest thing to Café Oto in America), Fire Museum Presents, the Rotunda in West Philly.\n\nOverrated: the Fillmore (an Ajax-factory Live Nation box), TLA (fine, grim South Street), anything at Xfinity Live.\n\nRadio and routing: WXPN (88.5, out of Penn) is the radio station that made the scene; its Free at Noon shows at World Cafe Live are exactly that. Bands routing between New York and DC nearly always stop here, and tickets are half London prices.',
